<Learn
  title="The monsoon and food prices · India Atlas"
  description="The monsoon and food prices: a failed monsoon reaches the inflation print three to six months late, and a good one shows up as cheap food the winter after. Food is nearly half the basket, so weather becomes monetary news."
  slug="monsoon-and-food-prices"
  kicker="Prices: The monsoon and food prices"
  h1="A failed monsoon reaches the print <i>three to six months late.</i>"
  lede="Food is nearly half the consumer basket, and most of that food grows under four months of rain. The weather inside the inflation number: the lag, the spikes, and the relief that arrives on the same delay as the pain."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "The monsoon & food prices"],
    ["Illustrated with", "Food CPI, 2016–2025"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO, IMD"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "7 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Monsoon & food prices"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>India's inflation number has weather inside it. Food carries 45.9 per cent of the consumer basket by weight, and most of that food grows under a sky that delivers the bulk of the year's rain in four months. When the monsoon fails, the failure does not appear in the data the week it happens. It arrives later, at harvest, wearing the disguise of an onion.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Monsoon <span class="sub">the rain that sets the food calendar</span></div>
          <p>The seasonal wind that brings most of India's rain between June and September. Cropping is organised around it: kharif crops, such as rice and pulses, are sown with the summer rains and harvested in autumn; rabi crops, such as wheat, are sown in winter on stored moisture and harvested in spring.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> one rainy season feeds two harvests, and both harvests feed the index.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">The lag is mechanical. Rain falls, or fails, from June to September. The kharif harvest comes in from October, shortages reach the mandis over the following weeks, and the year-on-year print, which compares months twelve apart, keeps moving long after the skies have cleared. A bad monsoon in July is a food inflation story by winter.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>The lag runs three to six months.</b> Rain in July; shortage at harvest; the print moves by winter. Weather becomes monetary news on a delay.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Food is 45.9 per cent of the basket.</b> Few economies of this size let the sky move the policy discussion so directly.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Good rain is news too.</b> A good monsoon shows up as cheap food the winter after. Relief arrives on the same delay as pain.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate : the sky in the data -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The sky, visible in the data</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Food y/y %, monthly</span></div>
    <p>Below is the food inflation rate for a decade, with the three episodes that teach the mechanism: the onion spike of December 2019, the vegetable spike of July 2023, and the deflation of 2025. One mechanism, three moments.</p>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-monsoon" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Rain in July, the print by winter: the lag, drawn once</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI food &amp; beverages, year-on-year, shaded: the 2019 monsoon months, Jan 2016 to Dec 2025</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-mo">–</div><div class="c">Dec 2025, food y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-monsoon"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, CPI food &amp; beverages group, base 2012=100, vintage <span id="pf-mo">–</span>, episodes dated by the prints they produced</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is the food inflation rate, the series that the basket's 45.9 per cent weight gives its power to. Read the three marked episodes as one mechanism. The shaded months are the 2019 monsoon; the spike marked beside them is its harvest, arriving at the December print. The 2023 spike was an erratic monsoon arriving almost in real time. The readings below zero in 2025 are the unwinding: a good harvest landing the winter after.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2019 onion case</span>
            <p>The 2019 monsoon was late, and the unseasonal autumn rain that followed it rotted stored onions across the growing belt. By December 2019 food inflation had reached <b id="mo-onion">–</b>, the decade's highest reading, months after the rain had done its damage. Nothing about the December economy explained the December print. The explanation had fallen from the sky earlier that year.</p>
            <p>The mirror image came six years on: after the spike of 2023 and a normal monsoon, food inflation fell through zero and bottomed at <b id="mo-defl">–</b> in October 2025, closing the year at <b id="mo-dec25">–</b>. The same lag delivered relief that had once delivered pain.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 why this is monetary news -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>Why weather becomes monetary news</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">The denominator under half the basket</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Because food is 45.9 per cent of the basket, the sky gets a vote in monetary policy. A vegetable spike can push headline inflation through the ceiling of the RBI's band by itself; a winter glut can pull it below the floor. The central bank cannot make it rain. It can only decide how much of the shock to tolerate and how much to lean against with interest rates.</p>
        <p>That is why forecasters in India read rainfall bulletins alongside bond yields, and why the companion entries on <a href="/learn/seasonality/">seasonality</a>, <a href="/learn/index-weights/">index weights</a> and <a href="/learn/inflation-targeting/">inflation targeting</a> belong on the same shelf as this one. The monsoon is the denominator under half the basket.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iv</span>
          <b>The sky gets a vote, not a veto.</b> Policy, buffers and trade can blunt a bad monsoon at the margin. They cannot repeal it.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Food inflation is the government's fault.”</h3>
          <p>Sometimes, in part: export bans, stock limits and import tariffs do move prices at the margin. But the first draft of every food print is written by the weather, months earlier. Blame and credit both arrive late.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“One good monsoon fixes food prices.”</h3>
          <p>It fixes the winter after, once. The harvest that follows good rain lowers the level a single time; it does not stop the level from climbing again the summer after that. Food relief has an expiry date, and it is usually the next monsoon.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Food deflation is pure relief.”</h3>
          <p>Relief at the shop can be distress at the farm gate. When vegetables sell below the cost of growing them, the household budget smiles and the farm budget bleeds. The print records both as a single minus sign.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Consumer Price Index group indices on base 2012=100, rural, urban and combined; retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage 2026-08-04. The food year-on-year rate is computed from the published food &amp; beverages group index. Rainfall narrative: India Meteorological Department seasonal summaries.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Food here is the CPI food &amp; beverages group, 45.9 per cent of the combined basket by weight. The lag of three to six months is descriptive, drawn from the episodes marked on the chart; it is not a forecasting rule.</p>
    <p>Where the data cannot answer a question, this page says so rather than inventing precision.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Prices, version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. The 2019, 2023 and 2025 episodes are used as history, not news; the chart redraws from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion guide: <a href="/guides/cpi/">Consumer Price Index</a>.</p>
  </div>

